# React & Component Rules

## Server vs Client Components

```typescript
// TEACHING: Server Components (default in App Router)
// - Can directly fetch data, access DB, read files
// - Cannot use hooks, browser APIs, or event handlers
// - Render on server, send HTML to client (faster initial load)

// This is a Server Component by default
export default async function EventList() {
  // TEACHING: Direct DB access is fine here - runs on server only
  const events = await db.select().from(event);
  return <ul>{events.map(e => <li key={e.id}>{e.title}</li>)}</ul>;
}

// TEACHING: Client Components - add "use client" directive
// - Can use hooks, state, effects, event handlers
// - Bundle is sent to client (increases JS payload)
"use client";

export function LikeButton({ eventId }: { eventId: string }) {
  // TEACHING: useState only works in Client Components
  const [liked, setLiked] = useState(false);
  return <button onClick={() => setLiked(!liked)}>Like</button>;
}
```

## Component Organization

- **UI primitives**: `src/components/ui/` (shadcn/ui components)
- **Feature components**: `src/components/[feature]/`
- **Page-specific**: Co-locate with page in `src/app/`

## Styling with Tailwind CSS 4

```typescript
// TEACHING: Use Tailwind utilities, avoid inline styles
// Tailwind purges unused styles, so bundle stays small

// GOOD - Tailwind utilities
<button className="bg-primary text-primary-foreground hover:bg-primary/90 px-4 py-2 rounded-md">

// BAD - Inline styles (not purged, harder to maintain)
<button style={{ backgroundColor: 'blue', padding: '8px 16px' }}>

// TEACHING: Use cn() utility for conditional classes
import { cn } from "@/lib/utils";

<div className={cn(
  "base-classes here",
  isActive && "active-classes",
  variant === "outline" && "border border-input"
)}>
```

## shadcn/ui Components - ALWAYS USE THESE

```typescript
// TEACHING: shadcn/ui components are copied to src/components/ui/
// You OWN this code - customize freely, don't treat as external lib
// IMPORTANT: Always use these components instead of building from scratch!

// Available components in src/components/ui/:
// - Card, CardHeader, CardTitle, CardDescription, CardContent, CardFooter, CardAction
// - Button (variants: default, secondary, outline, ghost, destructive)
// - Badge (variants: default, secondary, outline, destructive)
// - Avatar, AvatarImage, AvatarFallback
// - Input, Textarea, Label, Checkbox, Select
// - Dialog, Sheet, Drawer
// - Table, Tabs, Separator
// - DropdownMenu, Tooltip, Popover
// - Skeleton (for loading states)
// - Calendar (date picker with react-day-picker)
// - Collapsible (expandable sections)
// - Kbd, KbdGroup (keyboard shortcut display)
// - Breadcrumb (navigation breadcrumbs)
// - Toggle, ToggleGroup (toggle switches)
// - StatefulButton (animated button with loading/success)
// - Sidebar (navigation sidebar)
// - Sonner (toast notifications - use `toast` from sonner)
//
// Custom components (not in ui/):
// - Editor from @/components/rich-text-editor/Editor - TipTap rich text
// - Uploader from @/components/uploader - File upload
// - UserMenu from @/components/user-menu - User dropdown
// - StepProgress from @/components/step-progress - Multi-step progress

import { Button } from "@/components/ui/button";
import { Input } from "@/components/ui/input";
import { Card, CardHeader, CardTitle, CardContent } from "@/components/ui/card";
import { Badge } from "@/components/ui/badge";
import {
  Dialog,
  DialogContent,
  DialogHeader,
  DialogTitle,
} from "@/components/ui/dialog";

// TEACHING: Compose shadcn components, don't wrap unnecessarily
export function CreateEventDialog({ open, onOpenChange }) {
  return (
    <Dialog open={open} onOpenChange={onOpenChange}>
      <DialogContent>
        <DialogHeader>
          <DialogTitle>Create Event</DialogTitle>
        </DialogHeader>
        {/* Form content */}
      </DialogContent>
    </Dialog>
  );
}

// GOOD - Use Card components for content sections
<Card className="rounded-3xl shadow-lg">
  <CardHeader>
    <CardTitle>Section Title</CardTitle>
  </CardHeader>
  <CardContent>
    <Badge variant="secondary">Status</Badge>
  </CardContent>
</Card>

// BAD - Building cards from scratch with plain divs
<div className="border rounded-3xl shadow-lg">
  <div className="px-6 py-4 border-b">
    <h2>Section Title</h2>
  </div>
  <div className="p-6">
    <span className="px-2 py-1 rounded bg-muted">Status</span>
  </div>
</div>
```

## Color Palette - Use Semantic Colors

```typescript
// TEACHING: Colors are defined in src/styles/index.css
// Always use CSS custom properties via Tailwind classes for dark mode support

// Semantic color tokens available:
// --background / --foreground     - Page background and text
// --card / --card-foreground      - Card surfaces
// --primary / --primary-foreground - Primary actions, links
// --secondary / --secondary-foreground - Secondary elements
// --muted / --muted-foreground    - Subtle backgrounds, secondary text
// --accent / --accent-foreground  - Highlighted elements
// --destructive / --destructive-foreground - Errors, delete actions
// --border                        - Borders and dividers
// --input                         - Form input borders
// --ring                          - Focus rings
// --chart-1 to --chart-5          - Data visualization

// GOOD - Use semantic Tailwind color classes
<div className="bg-background text-foreground">
<div className="bg-card border-border">
<button className="bg-primary text-primary-foreground">
<span className="text-muted-foreground">
<div className="bg-primary/10 text-primary">  // With opacity modifier

// BAD - Hardcoded colors break dark mode and consistency
<div className="bg-white text-black">
<div className="bg-gray-100 border-gray-200">
<button className="bg-purple-600 text-white">
```

## Performance Rules

1. **Minimize "use client"** - Keep client components small and leaf-level
2. **Avoid prop drilling** - Use composition or context
3. **Lazy load** heavy components with `dynamic()`
4. **Memoize expensive** calculations with `useMemo`
5. **Use React Compiler** - It handles most optimizations automatically

## Accessibility

1. **Always use semantic HTML** - `<button>`, `<nav>`, `<main>`, etc.
2. **Add aria-labels** to icon-only buttons
3. **Ensure keyboard navigation** works
4. **Test with screen readers** for important flows

## Rich Text Editor

```typescript
// TEACHING: Use TipTap-based editor for rich text content
// Content is stored as JSONContent (JSONB in database)
// Max payload: 100KB for bigDescription fields

import Editor from "@/components/rich-text-editor/Editor";
import type { JSONContent } from "@tiptap/react";

// Editable mode - for forms
<Editor
  content={initialContent}       // Initial content to load
  value={controlledValue}        // Controlled value
  onChange={(json) => setContent(json)}
/>

// Read-only mode - for displaying saved content
<Editor
  content={savedContent}
  value={savedContent}
  readOnly
/>
```

